TASK:{"title":"AERO9500 Final Report (Part 2) - Space Mission Design","overview":"Design a simple space mission benefiting Australia and identify main elements of its space segment to demonstrate understanding of space segment systems (launch, orbits etc.) by developing a preliminary plan for a new mission using industry software and hand calculations."}
RUBRIC:[{"id":"C1","name":"Introduction: mission concept and rationale","weight":10,"max":1,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Addresses all requirements to a high standard. Detailed descriptions and discussions included, with references to literature as appropriate."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Addresses some requirements. Descriptions may lack specific details and/or referencing incomplete."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Many or all requirements not addressed. Poor descriptions or lack of appropriate referencing."}]},{"id":"C2","name":"Main body: mission design","weight":60,"max":6,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Demonstrates high-level understanding of design procedure. Design clearly addresses mission and course concepts. All major requirements addressed and well supported with quality evidence e.g. screenshots, software files, hand calcs, literature. Content comprehensive, accurate, persuasive."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Demonstrates adequate understanding of design procedure. Design may be inconsistent regarding purpose and clarity or feature errors. Major requirements addressed but may lack detail or not well supported with evidence. Content may not be comprehensive or persuasive."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Demonstrates minimal understanding of design procedure. Major requirements not addressed and/or lack evidence. Content incomplete or not persuasive."}]},{"id":"C3","name":"Conclusion","weight":10,"max":1,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Detailed and appropriate conclusions reached from evidence provided in report."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Some conclusions reached but may be generic or lack specific detail."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"No actual or useful conclusions reached."}]},{"id":"C4","name":"Report presentation","weight":20,"max":2,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Clear logical engineering report structure. Easy to read: well-written, good spelling, grammar, appropriate style. Consistent formatting. Executive summary/abstract provides concise overview. Graphical/tablular data appropriate, clear, consistent. References in-text match reference list and properly cited."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Not at professional level but uses headings/subheadings. May be difficult to read, writing just ok, spelling/grammar flaws, language style not quite appropriate. Figures/tables labelled but formatting unclear/inconsistent, reader may lose context. References mostly match list and mostly cited correctly."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Poorly structured, not coherent, shows lack of understanding of section purpose. Hard to read: poor writing, many spelling/grammar mistakes, possibly inappropriate informal style. Presentation poor impedes reading. Examples: inconsistent formatting, unlabelled figures/tables. References not cited or cited inconsistently."}]}]
MARKING:{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","met":"yes","page":4},{"id":"R2","met":"yes","page":5},{"id":"R3","met":"yes","page":5},{"id":"R4","met":"yes","page":6},{"id":"R5","met":"yes","page":9},{"id":"R6","met":"yes","page":10},{"id":"R7","met":"yes","page":13},{"id":"R8","met":"yes","page":14},{"id":"R9","met":"yes","page":17},{"id":"R10","met":"yes","page":14}],"technical_issues":[{"page":7,"issue":"Uses Wikipedia 'Timeline of spaceflight' as primary source for launch trade despite instruction Wikipedia not proper source."},{"page":11,"issue":"Sun-sync inclination vs altitude plot presented without hand calculation of formula i = cos^-1(-2*a^3.5 * RAANdot / (3*J2*R^2*sqrt(mu))) ; value 97.7924 deg stated but derivation missing."},{"page":13,"issue":"RAAN described as location of launch facility (27.33 deg) - incorrect; RAAN is orbit orientation, not launch longitude."},{"page":17,"issue":"No orbital maneuver delta-V analysis; claims direct injection but no transfer or maintenance budget."}],"overall":"Solid simple Australian-benefit mission with good STK evidence and end-to-end design; minor technical gaps and referencing issues but meets all Part 2 deliverables.","criteria":[{"id":"C1","level":"Accomplished","mark":8.0,"scale":10.0,"points":0.8,"max":1.0,"reasoning":"Mission concept clearly benefits Australia, primary mission fluid-lensing coral monitoring defined, single-sat justification and users (CSIRO/BOM) given with references. Detailed rationale.","evidence":[{"page":5,"quote":"Coral reefs, specifically the Great Barrier Reef, would immensely benefit from dedicated satellite monitoring."},{"page":4,"quote":"Australia's Great Barrier Reef is the largest coral reef in the world, spread over 344,400 km2."}]},{"id":"C2","level":"Accomplished","mark":8.0,"scale":10.0,"points":4.8,"max":6.0,"reasoning":"All segments covered: launch piggyback Vega E Kourou 5.05N, SSO 600km 97.79deg with COE table, STK 3D views and access report, Mount Pleasant ground station, 5-yr duration and drag-sail EOL. Good screenshots and references.","evidence":[{"page":9,"quote":"The proposed satellite will thus use the Vega E rocket as a launch vehicle"},{"page":12,"quote":"Semi-major axis (a) 6971km Earth radius of 6371km plus a proposed"},{"page":14,"quote":"Systems Tool Kit 11 (STK) was used to calculate access periods as seen below:"}]},{"id":"C3","level":"Accomplished","mark":7.0,"scale":10.0,"points":0.7,"max":1.0,"reasoning":"Conclusion summarizes launch, orbit, ground, and goal, ties back to coral bleaching problem, notes Vega E vs C alternative.","evidence":[{"page":18,"quote":"After analysis of each mission segment, a fully described satellite configuration has been constructed."},{"page":18,"quote":"A polar sun-synchronous orbit allows for consistent illumination of the oceans while maintaining a constant sun angle."}]},{"id":"C4","level":"Accomplished","mark":7.0,"scale":10.0,"points":1.4,"max":2.0,"reasoning":"Logical structure, executive summary, headings, labeled figures. Some formatting errors, Wikipedia used despite ban, bibliography messy but mostly cited.","evidence":[{"page":2,"quote":"This report outlines the intended designs and procedures involved in the construction of a Earth observation satellite"},{"page":10,"quote":"STK 3D view of orbit: 12 hours apart"}]}]}
